fix(hook): tell a tree with nothing in it from one that could not be read (#284)

The stop asked how much a run had left uncommitted through a pipe:

  dirty="$(git -C "$DIR" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"

and a pipeline reports the LAST command's status. `wc -l` always succeeds, so a
`git status` that failed printed nothing, counted 0, and was indistinguishable
from a clean tree. The hook then concluded there was nothing to capture. No
capture was attempted, so none failed, so #277's record was never written: the
run stopped, its work stayed in the tree, and nothing anywhere said so.

It is #277's defect one level out and harder to see, because the hook did
exactly what its code said. The measurement that catches it is the one this
repository already treats as load-bearing elsewhere -- verify-branch.sh was
changed in #281 for reporting "could not check" as "failed", and
no-personal-identity.sh refuses rather than passes when its configuration is
absent.

read_tree takes the status WITHOUT a pipe, so the exit code is git's own, and
counts lines from a value already in hand. A failure is now an outcome with its
own record: `tree_unreadable`, carrying what git said -- "fatal: not a git
repository" and "index.lock exists" are different problems with different
remedies, and that sentence went to a stderr the harness discards for a stop.

AND THE TREE THAT IS GONE, which is the first cause the issue names and never
reached the `git status` at all: `[ -d "$DIR" ]` excluded it one line earlier
and the stop walked past in silence. That is `tree_absent`.

It is asked BEFORE the commit count, deliberately. A run that has committed once
may still have left work uncommitted, so the commit count answers a different
question and cannot stand in for this one. What is recorded is not "there was
work" but that nobody knows.

The marker's task, worktree and agent type are read before any branch decides
anything. They used to be read inside the capture branch -- the one branch not
taken when the tree cannot be read -- so a record written on either new path
would have named no worktree, and a record missing the field a reader
correlates on has to be correlated by hand.

A tree read successfully and found clean still writes nothing, which is what
keeps the record worth reading. Both paths exit 0 and touch no index.

Driven by OPS-089 in scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh, written first and observed
red against the shim as it stood: 7 new assertions, all 7 red and red on merit
-- 0 records, every field empty, because there was nothing of any kind to read
-- while the 76 pre-existing cases stayed green in both directions. 88 passed,
0 failed after. The shim was driven under sh, bash, zsh and dash over an
unreadable tree: all four exited 0 and wrote records identical field for field
apart from the second the clock read.

